.TH NEWS 1
.SH NAME
news \- print news items
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B news
[
.B -a
|
.B -n
] [
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I News
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR news (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/news.c ,
239 lines), compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
With no arguments,
.I news
prints every item under
.B /lib/news
whose modification time is newer than the last time
.I news
was run by this user, newest first, and then records the current time
as the new "last read" mark. With
.B -a
it prints every item regardless of that mark, without updating it.
With
.B -n
it prints only the names of new items, one line prefixed
.BR news: ,
also without updating the mark. Given explicit
.I file
arguments, it prints exactly those items from
.B /lib/news
in the order given, ignoring the modification-time scan entirely.
.PP
Two names, "core" and "dead.letter", are never listed even if present
and new - the same fixed ignore list the real source has.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/news
directory of news items; hardcoded, like the real source - see BUGS.
.TP
.B $home/lib/newstime
per-user marker of when news was last read; its modification time (not
its content) is what matters. Only touched when
.B -a
and
.B -n
are absent.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/news.b
(translated from
.BR news.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR newstest (1)
.SH BUGS
The real source has no flag to point
.B /lib/news
or
.B $home/lib/newstime
anywhere else - both paths are hardcoded absolute paths in the C, not
parameters, and this translation keeps them exactly that way rather
than inventing an override the real tool never had. This makes testing
this program a real-namespace-fixture exercise; see
.IR newstest (1)
for how that is done without touching any real content.
.PP
The real source's own directory-sort comparator
(qsort with a hand-rolled
.B fcmp
that treats larger modification time as sorting first) is replaced
here by
.BR Readdir 's
.B MTIME
sort key - deliberately
.I not
.B MTIME|DESCENDING,
despite that flag's name: a standalone probe (three files, distinct
modification times) showed plain
.B MTIME
gives newest-first here and
.B MTIME|DESCENDING
gives oldest-first, the reverse of what the name suggests. Not this
tree's bug to fix, just confirmed by testing rather than assumed from
the flag's name - see
.B news.b's
own header comment.
.PP
.BR asctime (2)'s
own output format (space-padded day, no time zone field, and a
trailing newline that is part of the returned string, not added by the
caller) is reproduced by hand from
.BR Daytime->local() 's
field breakdown, not by calling
.BR Daytime->text() ,
whose own format differs in exactly those three ways.
